          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "An HbA1c result of 7.2% is above the general target for many non-pregnant adults with diabetes, which is typically described as below 7% (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), `guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets`). This result reflects the average blood glucose level over approximately the past three months (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), \"Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals\"). This general target aims to reduce certain complications of diabetes over time (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), `guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets`).\n\nGuidance from the National diabetes association standards of care recommends an A1c goal of below 7% for many nonpregnant adults, typically for those with a shorter duration of diabetes, longer life expectancy, and no significant cardiovascular disease (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), \"Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals\"). For some individuals, a more stringent goal, such as below 6.5%, might be considered if it can be achieved without significant low blood sugar or other harm (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), `guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets`). A less stringent goal, such as below 8%, may be appropriate for individuals with a history of severe low blood sugar, limited life expectancy, advanced health complications, extensive other illnesses, or when the 7% target is difficult to achieve despite appropriate management (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), \"Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals\").\n\nThe ICD-10-CM code for Type 2 diabetes mellitus is E11 (US clinical modification of ICD-10 maintained by NCHS and CMS, `coding-type-2-diabetes-e11`). This code is found in Chapter 4, which covers endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (US clinical modification of ICD-10 maintained by NCHS and CMS, `coding-icd10-chapter-04-endocrine`).\n\nYou should discuss this result with your clinician to understand what it means for your individual health and care plan (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), `guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets`). You might also discuss with your clinician what your individual HbA1c target should be, based on your overall health, other medical conditions, and treatment history (National diabetes association standards of care (educational summary), \"Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
